 03_8	About seventy-one. Sixty, plus one average gap of eleven.
 03_9	That's a recipe. Take the largest number and add the average gap. If the handful is fair, this recipe is right on average.
 03_10	Right on average is a promise about the recipe, not about seventy-one. We'll prove it, and then find out what it can't promise.
+04_1	Let's pin the question down. Call the true number of boxes the count. We don't know it. It might be sixty, seventy-one or five hundred.
+04_2	The friends' five boxes are a single handful. We assume the bin from before, so every handful of five was equally likely to be theirs.
+04_3	A recipe is any rule that turns a handful into an estimate of the count. Statisticians call it an estimator.
+04_4	Ours, the gap recipe, adds the average gap to the largest number. Of the sixty boxes from one to sixty, five are the friends', so the gaps hold sixty minus five, fifty-five.
+04_5	With any five numbers, the gaps below the largest hold the largest minus five. So the estimate is the largest, plus the largest minus five, over five. For our friends, sixty plus fifty-five over five, seventy-one.
+04_6	What would the recipe say if the five numbers were one, two, three, four and five?
+04_7	Five. There are no gaps, so there's nothing to add. And since gaps are never negative, the recipe never guesses below the largest number it saw.
+04_8	A single handful can't tell whether a recipe is good. We'll judge a recipe across every handful the company could have sold.
+05_1	A company of seventy-one boxes has over thirteen million handfuls of five, far too many to look at. So let's shrink the company. Suppose it made just five boxes, and you see two.
+05_2	There are ten possible pairs, from one and two up to four and five. In a fair draw, each pair is equally likely.
+05_3	Take the pair two and five. Below five, the unseen boxes are one, three and four. That's three boxes, five minus two, in two gaps. So with a pair, the recipe adds half of the largest minus two.
+05_4	For two and five, that's five plus three halves, six and a half. For one and two, there are no gaps, so the estimate is just two.
+05_5	Here are all ten estimates. Some are too low and some too high. What do the ten average?
+05_6	Exactly five, the true count. The largest number on its own averages only four.
+05_7	A recipe is unbiased if its estimates average to the true count, whatever that count is. A single small company isn't a proof. So why does adding the average gap work?
